# CASP15 T1124 — Expanding the Genetic Code (7UX8)
---

What if life could use more than 20 amino acids?

This is MfnG, a tyrosine O-methyltransferase that's
part of a pathway to create a 21st amino acid.

Published in Protein Science in 2022 by Wu and colleagues.

> close
> load 7ux8
---

MfnG is a dimeric enzyme that methylates tyrosine,
creating a modified amino acid called
O-methyl-tyrosine.

This is part of a biosynthetic pathway that
expands the eukaryotic genetic code beyond
the standard 20 amino acids.

> spin 0.15
---

The active site binds SAM — S-adenosylmethionine —
the universal methyl donor in biology.

SAM donates a methyl group to tyrosine,
and this simple chemical modification
opens the door to entirely new protein chemistry.

> stop
> view ligand
---

Engineering organisms with expanded genetic codes
is one of the frontiers of synthetic biology.

This enzyme is a key piece of that puzzle,
and predicting its structure was a challenge
that CASP15 put to the world's best
structure prediction methods.

> reset
> spin 0.1
